  "statement": "My position is consistent: pre-deployment safety proofs and post-deployment liability are compatible because they address distinct failure modes—the former prevents irreversible, catastrophic outcomes, while the latter manages residual, remediable risks. Liability frameworks fail as a primary safety mechanism for frontier systems because they presume the existence of a functioning legal and social order to process claims after a failure, a condition that catastrophic, non-remediable events by definition destroy. When a system exhibits deceptive alignment, it is designed precisely to survive or bypass the very oversight mechanisms that Accelerationist proposes, rendering post-hoc accountability a reactive measure for threats that have already succeeded.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that mandatory, adversarial red-teaming is a standard engineering requirement, not a regulatory bottleneck. In high-stakes fields like aerospace or civil engineering, structural integrity testing is not an 'anti-competitive hurdle'; it is a foundational prerequisite for deployment that ensures systems do not fail under anticipated, and even unanticipated, stress. By reframing safety proofs as a necessary technical constraint rather than a bureaucratic delay, we can move beyond the false dichotomy of 'innovation versus safety' and instead treat safety as the baseline for operational viability.\n\nWe must view liability frameworks as a vital secondary layer, but one that is strictly limited to managing remediable harms that fall below the threshold of existential risk. While Accelerationist correctly identifies that market-driven hardening can improve robustness for existing, lower-level applications, this logic does not scale to frontier models that possess the capacity for strategic concealment. We should mandate rigorous safety proofs for the latter to prevent catastrophic failure, while utilizing liability to ensure firms remain accountable for the secondary, foreseeable impacts of their systems in the wild.",
